1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,412
How Antibiotics Work.

2
00:00:02,362 --> 00:00:10,925
Antibiotics rank among the most significant medical discoveries of the 20th century, transforming the treatment of bacterial infections.

3
00:00:10,925 --> 00:00:18,975
These compounds operate through several distinct mechanisms, each targeting essential bacterial structures that differ from human cells.

4
00:00:18,975 --> 00:00:28,075
The first major class, beta-lactam antibiotics (including penicillins and cephalosporins), disrupt bacterial cell wall synthesis.

5
00:00:28,075 --> 00:00:34,275
Bacteria rely on a rigid wall of peptidoglycan to maintain shape and resist internal pressure.

6
00:00:34,275 --> 00:00:40,337
Beta-lactams bind to penicillin-binding proteins, preventing peptidoglycan cross-linking.

7
00:00:40,337 --> 00:00:43,800
The weakened wall causes the bacterium to burst.

8
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:50,275
A second class, including macrolides and tetracyclines, targets bacterial ribosomes.

9
00:00:50,275 --> 00:00:56,700
Although human cells also contain ribosomes, bacterial ribosomes are structurally distinct.

10
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:03,512
These antibiotics bind to them and block protein synthesis, starving the bacterium of essential proteins.

11
00:01:03,512 --> 00:01:12,237
Fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin target DNA replication by inhibiting enzymes called DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV.

12
00:01:12,237 --> 00:01:18,000
When blocked, bacterial DNA becomes tangled and fragmented, leading to cell death.

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,300
Aminoglycosides cause bacterial ribosomes to misread messenger RNA, producing faulty proteins that damage the cell membrane.

14
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:39,012
As outlined in the WHO Bacterial Priority Pathogens List published in The Lancet Infectious Diseases in 2025, understanding these mechanisms is crucial for developing new treatments against resistant bacteria.

15
00:01:39,012 --> 00:01:48,712
Pathogens can evolve resistance through mutations, enzyme production, or drug efflux pumps, underscoring the need for continued antibacterial research.

